of revenue maximisation. Refer to the previous diagram. There is a reduction in the demand for pizza. Ceteris paribus, which one of the following will happen to the restaurant’s average revenue and marginal revenue as a result of this change?","topics":["3-3-1-revenue","1-2-2-demand"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["average revenue","marginal revenue","fall in demand"],"qpPage":3,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":2,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 1 as printed","type":"mcq","options":["Average revenue and marginal revenue both fall","Average revenue and marginal revenue stay the same","Average revenue falls and marginal revenue increases","Average revenue increases and marginal revenue falls"],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2024-jun-q3a","p":5,"section":"A","questionNumber":"3(a)","parentQuestion":"3","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"Peloton is a firm which makes exercise bikes. Peloton’s objective is to prioritise selling fitness services rather than to achieve short-term profit maximisation. Draw a cost and revenue diagram to illustrate the different business objectives: profit maximisation and revenue maximisation.","topics":["3-2-1-business-objectives","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["profit maximisation","revenue maximisation","cost and revenue diagram","Peloton"],"qpPage":6,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2023-jun-q6e","p":4,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(e)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":15,"questionText":"Using examples from Figure 2 or Extract B, discuss the likely impact of price discrimination on a firm’s profits. Use a relevant diagram to support your answer.","topics":["3-4-5-monopoly","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["price discrimination","revenue","profits","monopoly"],"qpPage":15,"msPage":17,"ctxPage":13,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p3-2023-jun-q2c","p":22,"section":"B","questionNumber":"2(c)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":12,"questionText":"Discuss possible methods of price and non‑price competition that firms could use to increase the value of sales on the high street.","topics":["3-4-4-oligopoly","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["price competition","non-price competition","retail","revenue"],"qpPage":21,"msPage":20,"ctxPage":19,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2022-jun-q1a","p":3,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(a)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Disney+ is a streaming service that distributes films and television series produced by the Walt Disney Studios. Ceteris paribus, the most likely consequence of the above data is that Disney+ will experience an increase in its:","topics":["3-3-1-revenue","1-2-2-demand"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["revenue","increase in demand","Disney+"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["fixed costs","income elasticity of demand","price elasticity of demand","revenue"],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2021-nov-q4a","p":7,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(a)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"Patrick Street Productions produces musicals. Its latest production is ‘It’s a Wonderful Life’ and the total cost of this production is $200 000. The ticket price is $40. The theatre has a capacity of 300 seats. The company aims for revenue maximisation. If this is achieved, revenue from ticket sales will cover 30% of total costs. Charitable donations contribute 12.5% towards total cost and a government subsidy ensures the production covers all of its costs. Calculate the total revenue from ticket sales for ‘It’s a Wonderful Life’, assuming it is shown only five times, all at full capacity. You are advised to show your working.","topics":["3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["total revenue","calculation","theatre"],"qpPage":8,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2021-nov-q4c","p":7,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(c)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Patrick Street Productions produces musicals. Its latest production is ‘It’s a Wonderful Life’ and the total cost of this production is $200 000. The ticket price is $40. The theatre has a capacity of 300 seats. The company aims for revenue maximisation. If this is achieved, revenue from ticket sales will cover 30% of total costs. Charitable donations contribute 12.5% towards total cost and a government subsidy ensures the production covers all of its costs. Which one of the following conditions is necessary for revenue maximisation to occur?","topics":["3-2-1-business-objectives","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["revenue maximisation","marginal revenue","business objectives"],"qpPage":9,"msPage":9,"ctxPage":8,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 4 as printed","type":"mcq","options":["Average revenue equals average cost","Average revenue equals marginal cost","Marginal revenue equals average revenue","Marginal revenue equals zero"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2021-nov-q6e","p":7,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(e)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":15,"questionText":"With reference to the information provided, discuss the decision by Jet2 to increase its package holiday prices.","topics":["3-3-1-revenue","1-2-3-price-income-cross-elasticities-of-demand"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["pricing decision","revenue","elasticity","package holidays"],"qpPage":16,"msPage":19,"ctxPage":14,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2020-oct-q6e","p":8,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(e)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":15,"questionText":"Discuss the likely benefits of price discrimination to rail passengers. Use a diagram to support your answer.","topics":["3-4-5-monopoly","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["price discrimination","rail fares","consumer surplus","diagram"],"qpPage":11,"msPage":20,"ctxPage":9,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2019-jun-q4b","p":2,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(b)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"In 2016, the insurance group Esure undertook a demerger with its GoCompare price comparison website. Following the demerger, GoCompare announced in 2017 a profit of £17.5 million, up 21.5% on 2016. Total revenue in 2017 was £75.8 million, up 4.1% on 2016. Calculate, using the information provided, the total costs of GoCompare in 2016.","topics":["3-3-2-costs","3-3-4-normal-profits-supernormal-profits-losses","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["total costs","profit","total revenue","calculation"],"qpPage":7,"msPage":7,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2019-jun-q6d","p":2,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(d)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":12,"questionText":"Discuss one likely reason for the rise in BT’s profit (Figure 2, Extracts B and C). Use a cost and revenue diagram to support your answer.","topics":["3-3-4-normal-profits-supernormal-profits-losses","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["profit","cost and revenue diagram","BT","supernormal profit"],"qpPage":13,"msPage":14,"ctxPage":11,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2018-jun-q2a","p":1,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(a)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"The number of individual weekly ticket sales from UK National Lottery games operated by Camelot was 73 million in the financial year 2015–2016. The sale price of each lottery ticket was £2. This figure included 24 pence of tax revenue on each ticket sold. Calculate the weekly revenue received by Camelot after paying the tax to the government. You are advised to show your working.","topics":["1-2-9-indirect-taxes-subsidies","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["indirect tax","revenue","National Lottery","calculation"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2018-jun-q3a","p":1,"section":"A","questionNumber":"3(a)","parentQuestion":"3","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"The average fee charged by high-street estate agents to homeowners for selling property is 1.3% of the final sale price. Assume the selling fee is 1.3%. Calculate the payment received by an estate agent on a property sold for £489 000. You are advised to show your working.","topics":["3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["percentage calculation","estate agents","revenue"],"qpPage":7,"msPage":6,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2018-jun-q4b","p":1,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(b)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Emily owns and operates a nail ink salon. The diagram shows the cost and revenue curves for treatments at her nail ink salon. Initially, Emily sets her price to maximise profits. Emily now decides to change her objective from revenue maximisation to sales maximisation. This change will lead to:","topics":["3-2-1-business-objectives","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["sales maximisation","revenue maximisation","business objectives"],"qpPage":11,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":10,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 4 as printed","type":"mcq","options":["a decrease in the number of customers","a decrease in the price of treatments","an increase in productive efficiency","an increase in the level of profit"],"usesFigure":true},{"id":"edexcel-a-p3-2018-jun-q1b","p":19,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(b)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":12,"questionText":"With reference to Figure 3 and other information provided, discuss the price and non-price strategies that Starbucks may use to increase profitability.","topics":["3-4-4-oligopoly","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["price strategies","non-price competition","Starbucks","profitability"],"qpPage":6,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2017-jun-q2b","p":0,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(b)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"Between 2010 and 2015 the average price of tea in the UK increased from £7.20 per kilo to £8.48 per kilo. Over the same period the quantity of tea purchased fell from 97 million kilos to 76 million kilos. Calculate the change in total sales revenue for UK tea retailers between 2010 and 2015. You are advised to show your working.","topics":["1-2-3-price-income-cross-elasticities-of-demand","3-3-1-revenue"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-1","theme-3"],"keywords":["total revenue","price elasticity of demand","tea","calculation"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p1-2017-jun-q4a","p":0,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(a)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"In 2015 JCB, the construction equipment manufacturer, experienced a 6% fall in revenue. This resulted from a reduction in sales of construction equipment to emerging markets. Draw a cost and revenue diagram to show the likely impact of a reduction in sales of construction equipment on JCB’s profits.","topics":["3-3-4-normal-profits-supernormal-profits-losses","3-3-1-revenue","3-3-2-costs"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-3"],"keywords":["cost and revenue diagram","profit","fall in demand","JCB"],"qpPage":8,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null}]}
